   "subject": "Re: Fixed calibration - how to make mirt calibrate with MWU-MEM method",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Hugo,<div><br></div><div>I finally found a few hours (and more importantly, energy) to look into the details of the paper and how to perform these fixed calibration methods with mirt. It turns out that mirt is capable of executing each of these methods right now without any modifications to the package (I was surprised by this, actually, so it looks like my previous work exposing customized density/probability functions was more useful than I initially anticipated!). See the attached R script for a demonstration. This was a fun little exercise, and much of these estimation methods could certainly be rolled into a single function and distributed in mirt. I&#39;ll consider doing this in the future if researchers actually are interested in these types of fixed calibration methods (if you know any other methods that would fall within the IRT estimation framework feel free to send those along and I&#39;ll add them to my reading/implementation list). </div><div><br></div><div>As an aside, my general impression of these techniques at this point is only so-so. They feel quite ad-hoc, and even the most appealing &quot;Multiple Prior Weights Updating and Multiple EM Cycles (MWU-MEM)&quot; seems to lack some theoretical rigor in terms of the asymptotic performance. E.g., one can&#39;t obtain large sample SEs for the new item parameter estimates since the initial calibration parameters are treated as fixed and known, and even if the parameters were fixed but still included in the ACOV computation there&#39;s no way these algorithms are guaranteed to be at some maximum-likelihood point in the parameter space. The calibration with the empirically updated prior weights is also a function of the number of quadrature points used since these aren&#39;t &#39;free&#39; parameters (they cost Q - 1 degrees of freedom to estimate, which doesn&#39;t appear in the calibration results). I suppose one could do something like a bootstrap calibration to obtain better sampling variability estimates, or more efficiently some modification of a delta method, for example, but any method that doesn&#39;t give theoretically justifiable sampling variability estimates always puts me a little on edge....  Cheers.</div><div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
